      <!-- SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION & BASIC SLOPE -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          What is Slope? Understanding Rise Over Run
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          In mathematics, engineering, and architecture, <strong>slope</strong> is a measure of the steepness, incline, or gradient of a straight line, surface, or structural element. It describes the rate at which vertical elevation changes relative to horizontal progress. The vertical distance between two points is termed the <strong>rise</strong> (often denoted by <em>Δy</em>), while the horizontal distance traversed between those same two points is known as the <strong>run</strong> (denoted by <em>Δx</em>).
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          The mathematical definition of slope <em>m</em> is formulated as:
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/70 p-4 dark:border-blue-950 dark:bg-blue-950/40 text-center">
          <span class="text-base sm:text-lg font-black text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200 font-mono">
            Slope (m) = Rise / Run = Δy / Δx
          </span>
        </div>

        <!-- DIAGRAM 1: BASIC SLOPE -->
        <div class="my-6 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-3 shadow-md dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <img
            src="/images/math/basic-slope-diagram.svg"
            alt="Geometric right triangle diagram illustrating vertical rise, horizontal run, slope angle theta, and the slope equation m equals rise over run."
            class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="mt-2 text-center text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
            Figure 1: Fundamental slope geometry displaying the orthogonal relationship between vertical rise, horizontal run, and the inclined surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          A <strong>positive slope</strong> indicates an upward incline, where the elevation rises as you advance forward from left to right. Conversely, a <strong>negative slope</strong> represents a downward descent, where elevation decreases as horizontal distance increases. When the line is completely level, the rise is zero, resulting in a slope of zero (<span class="font-mono">m = 0</span>). When a line is purely vertical, horizontal distance is zero, yielding an undefined mathematical slope because division by zero is impossible in Euclidean geometry.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 2: SLOPE PERCENTAGE VS DEGREES -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Slope Percentage vs. Angle in Degrees
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Slope can be represented in multiple standard formats depending on the technical field:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-sm space-y-1.5 list-disc list-inside text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Decimal Slope:</strong> The pure numerical fraction <span class="font-mono font-bold">m = Rise / Run</span> (e.g., 0.25).</li>
          <li><strong>Slope Ratio:</strong> Expressed as <span class="font-mono font-bold">Rise : Run</span> or normalized to <span class="font-mono font-bold">1 : N</span> (e.g., 1 unit of rise for every 4 units of run).</li>
          <li><strong>Slope Percentage:</strong> The decimal slope multiplied by 100, representing the vertical rise per 100 units of horizontal run (<span class="font-mono font-bold">Slope % = (Rise / Run) × 100</span>).</li>
          <li><strong>Slope Angle (θ):</strong> The geometric angle in degrees formed between the inclined surface and the horizontal ground plane, computed via the arctangent function: <span class="font-mono font-bold">θ = arctan(Rise / Run)</span>.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- DIAGRAM 2: SLOPE PERCENTAGE VS ANGLE -->
        <div class="my-6 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-3 shadow-md dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <img
            src="/images/math/slope-percentage-angle-diagram.svg"
            alt="Comparison chart demonstrating that a 100 percent slope equals 45 degrees, 50 percent equals 26.6 degrees, and 200 percent equals 63.4 degrees."
            class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="mt-2 text-center text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
            Figure 2: Geometric comparison proving that 100% slope corresponds to 45°, and that slopes steeper than 45° exceed 100%.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Why 100% Slope Equals 45°, Not 90°
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          A widespread misconception in everyday conversation is assuming that a 100% slope represents a completely vertical cliff or wall (90°). In mathematics and civil engineering, <strong>a 100% slope means the vertical rise equals the horizontal run</strong> (1 meter of climb for every 1 meter of forward advance). In a right triangle where the opposite leg equals the adjacent leg, the angle is <span class="font-mono font-bold">arctan(1) = 45°</span>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Consequently, slopes can legitimately <strong>exceed 100%</strong> without violating any laws of geometry. A steep rock climbing wall, ski couloir, or steep structural roof can easily have an angle of 60°, which produces a slope percentage of <span class="font-mono font-bold">tan(60°) × 100 ≈ 173.2%</span>. As the inclination angle approaches 90°, the mathematical slope percentage grows toward infinity.
        </p>
      </section>

