      <!-- SECTION 6: COMMON MISTAKES -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Common Mistakes in Slope Calculations
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-red-950 dark:bg-red-950/30">
            <strong class="text-red-900 dark:text-red-300 block mb-1">1. Using Sloped Distance Instead of Horizontal Run:</strong>
            Slope is strictly vertical rise divided by horizontal ground distance. Dividing rise by the diagonal sloped length is a common error that computes the mathematical sine (sin θ) rather than the tangent (tan θ), producing an underestimated slope percentage.
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-red-950 dark:bg-red-950/30">
            <strong class="text-red-900 dark:text-red-300 block mb-1">2. Confusing Slope Percentage with Degrees:</strong>
            A 10% slope is NOT 10 degrees. An inclination of 10% corresponds to only about 5.71 degrees. Conversely, a 10-degree incline produces a 17.63% slope.
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-red-950 dark:bg-red-950/30">
            <strong class="text-red-900 dark:text-red-300 block mb-1">3. Mixing Measurement Units:</strong>
            Always ensure rise and run are expressed in identical units before dividing. If your rise is measured in inches and your run in feet, either convert the run to inches (multiply by 12) or the rise to feet (divide by 12).
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-red-950 dark:bg-red-950/30">
            <strong class="text-red-900 dark:text-red-300 block mb-1">4. Assuming 100% Slope is the Maximum Possible:</strong>
            100% slope simply denotes a 45-degree angle where rise equals run. Geometrically and physically, steeper inclines can exceed 100%, 200%, or more.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

